able def naimarkData {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkData_source {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkData_sum_some_le_one {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkData_isProj_sum_some {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\nnoncomputable def naimarkDilatedEffect {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedEffect_pos {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedEffect_sum {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedEffect_isProj {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\nnoncomputable def naimarkDilatedMeasurement {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedMeasurement_effect {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedMeasurement_isProjective {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkCompression_naimarkDilatedEffect {α d : Type} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\nnoncomputable def naimarkDilatedState (α : Type) [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedState_apply (α : Type) [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem inner_applyOperatorToState {ι : Type} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedState_apply_mul (α : Type) [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem inner_heteroKron_naimarkDilatedState (α : Type) [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]\ntheorem naimarkDilatedState_norm (α : Type) [Fins analysis.\n +\n +Take $m=d=1$, $\\eps=0$, and let $q$ tend to infinity through admissible field\n +sizes. The Pauli basis test has a perfect projective strategy by\n +\\path{lem:pauli-completeness}.\\footnote{See\n +\\path{references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex},\n +lines~1232--1255, for the statement and its projective Pauli measurements.}\n +On its expanded local spaces, choose the supplied joint point measurement on\n +both sides to have the deterministic answer $(x^2,0)$: the effect for this\n +answer is the identity, and every other effect is zero. This is a complete\n +projective measurement. Its self-consistency defect is zero. The ordered-product\n +distances are finite real numbers on the finitely many placements, so a common\n +scalar $\\delta_Q$ bounds all of them. Thus this family satisfies the supplied\n +witness hypotheses. No estimate of this scalar in terms of $\\eps$ is required.\n +\n +The extended point answer is $\\alpha x^2$. In dimension $2m+2=4$, the directly\n +indexed line-point law assigns weight $1/2$ to axis lines and then chooses one\n +of the four coordinates uniformly. Its X-axis branch therefore has mass $1/8$.\n +On that branch, $z,\\alpha,\\beta$ are fixed along the line, while $x$ is uniform\n +in the field. The canonical base has X coordinate zero, so the affine line\n +parameter is exactly $x$. For $\\alpha\\ne0$, every permitted line answer $p$\n +has degree at most one, and $p(X)-\\alpha X^2$ is a nonzero polynomial of degree\n +two. It has at most two roots, hence its agreement with the point answer is at\n +most $2/q$.\n +\n +This bound also holds for any line POVM. Indeed, for a fixed line let $w_p$\n +be the expectation of its effect for $p$ in the expanded state, at the line\n +player's placement. Positivity and completeness give $w_p\\ge0$ and\n +$\\sum_p w_p=1$; the axis-degree field makes $w_p=0$ for higher-degree answers.\n +The opposite point effect is deterministically the identity at $\\alpha x^2$,\n +so the agreement is precisely the $w_p$-weighted average of those root-count\n +fractions. These weights depend on the line but not on $x$ along it. Averaging\n +over the fixed coordinates, and using nonnegativity of the defect on the\n +remaining question branches, gives for either player orientation\n +\\[\n + \\operatorname{defect}\\ \\ge\\\n + \\frac18\\left(1-\\frac1q\\right)\\left(1-\\frac2q\\right).\n +\\]\n +There is no undefined evaluation on these nonzero axis directions, so the\n +completed answer alphabet does not change this calculation. The scalar field\n +identification preserves the polynomial evaluations and their root counts.\n +\n +The lower bound tends to $1/8$. In contrast, the predicate\n +\\path{IsPolyErr}\\textsubscript{2} bounds the proposed error at these parameters by\n +$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}(0,1/q)\\le Cq^{-s}$ with $s>0$, which tends to zero.\n +Consequently the unrestricted supplied-point auxiliary is false. This argument\n +does not refute the existential point-witness declaration or the source\n +\\path{lem:qld-4-13}; neither requires a line approximation to every arbitrarily\n +chosen point family.\n +\n +Two parts of the obstruction are independently verified in Lean: every\n +projective joint family admits some finite witness error, and a coefficient\n +answer with zero quadratic coefficient agrees with a nonzero multiple of the\n +square function with probability at most $2/q$.\\footnote{The declarations are\n +\\path{CombinedPointsWitness.exists_error_of_projective} and\n +\\path{linear_quadratic_agreement_le} in\n +\\path{MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/PointErrorObstruction.lean}. The first uses the\n +existence of an upper bound for a finite set of real numbers; the second reuses\n +\\path{directCoefficientCollision_avg_le}, which reduces to Mathlib's\n +univariate root count. The complete strategy-level contradiction is the\n +mathematical argument above, not a Lean counterexample theorem.}\n +The assigned signature and its unfinished proof are preserved, with this\n +obstruction tracked by \\ghissue{509}. A future realignment must retain the\n +polynomial point-error dependency or use the existentially constructed points.\n +Even after that domain repair, the separate printed-error estimate discussed\n +below remains open.\n +\n++||||||| ae124f8f\n++=======\n+ \\subsection{Unrestricted point witnesses in the extended-line assertion}\n+ \n+ The same obstruction applies to the auxiliary extended-line assertion when\n+ its input point error is an arbitrary scalar.  The first-route estimate\n+ $C m g(\\eps,md/q)$ does not remove this obstruction: it still tends to zero\n+ at $\\eps=0$ with fixed $m,d$ and increasing $q$.\n+ The source instead uses the points constructed in its preceding lemmas,\n+ with $\\delta_Q=\\poly(\\eps)$; Claim~17-1 explicitly retains this error.\n+ \\footnote{The unrestricted auxiliary is\n+ \\path{exists_extendedLinesWitness_established_ofPointsWitness} in\n+ \\path{MIPStarRE/QPBT/Combining/Apply.lean}, tracked by \\ghissue{511}.\n+ The source passages are \\path{lem:qld-4-10}, \\path{lem:qld-4-12}, and\n+ \\path{claim:17-1} in\n+ \\path{references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex},\n+ lines~689--709, 993--1018, and 1140--1173.}\n+ \n+ For an explicit example, set $m=d=1$ and let $q=2^{2k+1}>2$ increase.\n+ These parameters are admissible.  Use the perfect projective strategy from\n+ the completeness lemma for the Pauli basis test in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}, and\n+ let $\\hat\\psi$ be its normalized expanded state.\n+ \\footnote{The source is \\path{lem:pauli-completeness} in\n+ \\path{references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex},\n+ lines~1229--1421.}\n+ On each player's expanded\n+ local space, define the supplied joint point measurement by\n+ \\[\n+  Q^{x,z}_{a,b}=\n+  \\begin{cases}\n+    \\Id,&(a,b)=(x^2,0),\\\\\n+    0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n+  \\end{cases}\n+ \\]\n+ This is a projective measurement and is exactly self-consistent in every\n+ opposite placement.  The finitely many averaged squared distances from the\n+ two ordered expanded point products are finite real numbers.  Taking their\n+ maximum and zero gives a scalar $\\delta_Q$ satisfying all the fields of the\n+ point witness.  No smallness of this scalar is required by the unrestricted\n+ assertion.  The combined point answer at $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ is\n+ deterministically $\\alpha x^2$.\n+ \n+ The directly indexed law in dimension four chooses the axis component with\n+ probability $1/2$, and then chooses each coordinate with probability $1/4$.\n+ Restrict to its X-axis lines, of total mass $1/8$.  Such a line has fixed\n+ $(z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ and uniform coordinate $x$.  When $\\alpha\\ne0$, every\n+ line outcome with nonzero effect has degree at most one by the axis-degree\n+ requirement.  Consequently the polynomial $f(X)-\\alpha X^2$ is nonzero of\n+ degree two, so it vanishes at at most two field elements.  Averaging over a\n+ POVM does not increase the agreement bound $2/q$: for a fixed line its\n+ outcome weights are the nonnegative expectations of the placed effects in\n+ $\\hat\\psi$, sum to one, and do not depend on the subsequently sampled $x$.\n+ Since the opposite point effect is either the identity or zero, these\n+ weights give exactly the evaluated agreement probabilities.  On these\n+ nondegenerate axis lines every evaluation is determined, so the completed\n+ answer symbol causes no additional case.\n+ \n+ The coefficient $\\alpha$ is uniform and independent of $x$ on this part of\n+ the law.  All remaining consistency contributions are nonnegative.  Thus\n+ either orientation of an extended-line witness must have error at least\n+ \\[\n+  \\frac18\\left(1-\\frac1q\\right)\\left(1-\\frac2q\\right)\n+  \\longrightarrow \\frac18.\n+ \\]\n+ On the other hand, the poled point witness; the obligation below supplies it existentially. Relating\nits game to the source's seed-bearing game requires the transport and soundness\nobligations in\n`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility.tex`.\n\n**Error contract:** the polynomial bound printed in the source is carried\nby `IsPolyErr₂`, which states the corrected sum form\n`f x y ≤ C * (x ^ r + y ^ s)` with `1 ≤ C` and positive exponents on the\nclosed nonnegative quadrant, in place of the product form `C * (x * y) ^ C`\nof the source shorthand at `04_preliminaries.tex:22-29`.  The correction and\nthe two-dimensional strategy that refutes the product form are recorded in\n`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_pasting-product-error.tex` and tracked by issue #196