   5\\\\t\\\\n     6\\\\tReplaces `.github/workflows/pr-review.yml` — the `gate`, `code-review` and\\\\n     7\\\\t`prose-review` jobs — together with the review half of the `@claude` /\\\\n     8\\\\t`@codex` mention system documented in `docs/pr_review_management.md`.  The\\\\n     9\\\\tsubstantive review criteria are unchanged: `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md` §5 and the\\\\n    10\\\\tprompt pair under `.github/prompts/` are the same texts the GitHub jobs used.\\\\n    11\\\\tWhat changed is only who runs them and where the verdict lands.\\\\n    12\\\\t\\\\n    13\\\\t    local/bin/review.sh <pr-id> [--force-review] [--dry-run]\\\\n    14\\\\t\\\\n    15\\\\t---\\\\n    16\\\\t\\\\n    17\\\\t## 1. Why the reviewer is chained to CI\\\\n    18\\\\t\\\\n    19\\\\tThe parent workflow's own header records the reason (`pr-review.yml:3-8`):\\\\n    20\\\\tthe predecessors `claude-code-review.yml` and `blverbatim, and it recognises both providers and both fix\\\\n   128\\\\t   kinds.  `local/bin/autofix.sh` writes exactly `[codex-auto-fix]` and\\\\n   129\\\\t   `[codex-review-fix]` (DESIGN.md, \\\\\\\"Fix commits\\\\\\\").  Change either side without\\\\n   130\\\\t   the other and this guard fails open, silently.\\\\n   131\\\\t2. **The combined iteration cap (`autofix.md` §5).**  One counter across all\\\\n   132\\\\t   fix kinds, not one per kind.\\\\n   133\\\\t3. **The exclusion of sync and audit failures from auto-fix**\\\\n   134\\\\t   (`autofix.md` §3).\\\\n   135\\\\t\\\\n   136\\\\tThe guard has one deliberate hole.  `pr-review.yml:69-72` says: *we only want\\\\n   137\\\\tto review human-authored pushes and the final bot-fix result (detected by\\\\n   138\\\\titeration cap)*.  Without that exception, the last fix commit — the one that\\\\n   139\\\\tships — is the only commit on the branch nobody ever reviewed.  So `autofix.sh`\\\\n   140\\\\tcalls `review.sh <id> --force-review` once when the cap is reached — after\\\\n   141\\\\t**releasing its own fix lock** (`release_fix_lock` in `autofix.sh`), because\\\\n   142\\\\t`review.sh` refuses to run while the branch's fix lock has a live holder and\\\\n   143\\\\tthat holder would otherwise be the very process asking for the review.\\\\n   144\\\\t`--force-review` is the only way past rung 9.  Do not use it to \\\\\\\"just get a\\\\n   145\\\\treview\\\\\\\" of a bot commit; that reopens the cascade one commit at a time.\\\\n   146\\\\t\\\\n   147\\\\t## 6. What the reviewer must return\\\\n   148\\\\t\\\\n   149\\\\tThere is no GitHub review-state field to fall back on, so the verdict is a\\\\n   150\\\\ttrailer in the agent's last message (`codex exec -o <file>`), and the contract\\\\n   151\\\\tdemands three things in order:\\\\n   152\\\\t\\\\n   153\\\\t1. a `## Findings` section, one line per finding, in exactly this shape:\\\\n   154\\\\t\\\\n   155\\\\t       - [ ] F1 (blocker) `MIPStarRE/Path/File.lean:123` — one-line summary\\\\n   156\\\\t\\\\n   157\\\\t   with severity in {`blocker`, `changes`, `advisory`} and `-` in place of\\\\n   158\\\\t   `path:line` when a finding is not tied to a line; or the single line\\\\n   159\\\\t   `- none`;\\\\n   160\\\\t2. a `## Review` section with the prose;\\\\n   161\\\\t3. as the final line, alone:\\\\n   162\\\\t\\\\n   163\\\\t       VERDICT: APPROVED | COMMENTED | CHANGES_REQUESTED\\\\n   164\\\\t\\\\n   165\\\\tA missing or malformed trailer is **not** an approval: `review.sh` exits 4 and\\\\n   166\\\\tsets `review_state: blocked`, keeping the raw output under\\\\n   167\\\\t`~/.cache/mipstarre-dev/review/<pr>/<sha>/`.  Nothing in the findings section\\\\n   168\\\\tis discarded either — a line that does not parse is kept verbatim as a\\\\n   169\\\\t`changes`-severity finding labelled `unparsed finding:`, and a non-approving\\\\n   170\\\\tverdict with an empty ledger gets one synthesised finding so the merge gate\\\\n   171\\\\tstill blocks.  Both rules follow the same principle as rung 5: the failure mode\\\\n   172\\\\tworth engineering against is a review that reads green without having happened.\\\\n   173\\\\t\\\\n   174\\\\t## 7. Which reviews run\\\\n   175\\\\t\\\\n   176\\\\t* **Code review** always.  `pr-review.yml` ran it as a matrix over\\\\n   177\\\\t  `CLAUDE_CODE_REVIEW_PROVIDERS` (anthropic, deepseek).  Locally the matrix\\\\n   178\\\\t  collapses to one codex session; a second backend can be added by running\\\\n   179\\\\t  `review.sh` again with `MIPSTARRE_REVIEW_MODEL` set, which writes a separate\\\\n   180\\\\t  per-SHA file only if you also change the file name, so treat multi-provider\\\\n   181\\\\t  review as unimplemented rather than as a one-liner.\\\\n   182\\\\t* **Prose review** only when the diff touches `blueprint/`.  On GitHub it ran\\\\n   183\\\\t  unconditionally on a cheaper tier; gating it on the diff is a local\\\\n   184\\\\t  cost decision, not a weakening — the prose prompt reviews blueprint ↔ Lean\\\\n   185\\\\t  equivalence and blueprint prose, and a diff that touches no blueprint file\\\\n   186\\\\t  has nothing for it to review.  Set `MIPSTARRE_PROSE_MODEL` for the\\\\n   187\\\\t  cheaper-tier split.\\\\n   188\\\\t\\\\n   189\\\\tThe failure semantics of the two are deliberately different, and the difference\\\\n   190\\\\tis inherited: `pr-review.yml:112-131` *fails* the code review when its token is\\\\n   191\\\\tmissing, while `pr-review.yml:202-224` *skips* the prose review in the same\\\\n   192\\\\tsituation.  Locally, a code reviewer that dies without output blocks the PR; a\\\\n   193\\\\tprose reviewer that dies leaves a warning and the code verdict stands.\\\\n   194\\\\t\\\\n   195\\\\t`review_state` in `pr.md` takes the **worst** of the two verdicts, written\\\\n   196\\\\tverbatim: `APPROVED`, `COMMENTED` or `CHANGES_REQUESTED`.  The two states in\\\\n   197\\\\twhich there is no verdict are lowercase words rather than verdicts —\\\\n   198\\\\t`blocked` (the gate refused, or the reviewer produced nothing parseable) and\\\\n   199\\\\t`pending` (this SHA has not been reviewed; `autofix.sh` sets it after every fix\\\\n   200\\\\tcommit).  `local/bin/pr_merge.py` compares against exactly these strings:\\\\n   201\\\\tit merges on `APPROVED`, or on `COMMENTED` with an empty ledger, and refuses on\\\\n   202\\\\teverything else.\\\\n   203\\\\t\\\\n   204\\\\t## 8. Concurrency\\\\n   205\\\\t\\\\n   206\\\\t| Lock | Key | Cancellation |\\\\n   207\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\n   208\\\\t| review | PR id | none — a queued run waits, then re-checks the head |\\\\n   209\\\\t| fix (`autofix.md`) | branch | supersession sentinel |\\\\n   210\\\\t\\\\n   211\\\\tThe split of keys is inherited (`pr-review.yml:18-20` groups by PR number with\\\\n   212\\\\t`cancel-in-progress: false`; `auto-fix.yml:259-261` groups by head branch with\\\\n   213\\\\t`cancel-in-progress: true`) and it matters: cancelling a review wastes the\\\\n   214\\\\ttokens already spent and produces nothing, whereas cancelling a superseded fix\\\\n   215\\\\tsaves a write to a branch that has already moved.\\\\n   216\\\\t\\\\n   217\\\\tLocks are directories under `~/.cache/mipstarre-dev/locks/` holding the\\\\n   218\\\\tholder's pid — `flock(1)` does not exist on macOS.  A lock whose holder is gone\\\\n   219\\\\tis reclaimed.  After acquiring the review lock, `review.sh` re-reads\\\\n   220\\\\t`head_sha`: a fix commit that landed while this run queued invalidates the\\\\n   221\\\\treview, and the run exits without a verdict rather than describing a commit\\\\n   222\\\\tthat is no longer head.  The same check runs again after the agent returns; if\\\\n   223\\\\tthe head moved during the review, the per-SHA review file is still written (it\\\\n   224\\\\tis a true statement about that SHA) but `pr.md` is left alone.\\\\n   225\\\\t\\\\n   226\\\\t`review.sh` also refuses to start while a fix lock is held for the branch.\\\\n   227\\\\tThe two tools share one worktree here, where GitHub gave each job a fresh\\\\n   228\\\\tcheckout; without this cross-check the reviewer would read a tree being\\\\n   229\\\\trewritten under it.\\\\n   230\\\\t\\\\n   231\\\\t## 9. The findings ledger\\\\n   232\\\\t\\\\n   233\\  182\\\\t    holder=\\\\\\\"$(cat \\\\\\\"$dir/pid\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null || true)\\\\\\\"\\\\n   183\\\\t    if [ -n \\\\\\\"$holder\\\\\\\" ] && ! kill -0 \\\\\\\"$holder\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null; then\\\\n   184\\\\t      warn \\\\\\\"removing stale lock $dir (holder pid $holder is gone)\\\\\\\"\\\\n   185\\\\t      rm -rf \\\\\\\"$dir\\\\\\\"\\\\n   186\\\\t      continue\\\\n   187\\\\t    fi\\\\n   188\\\\t    if [ \\\\\\\"$waited\\\\\\\" -ge \\\\\\\"$wait_s\\\\\\\" ]; then\\\\n   189\\\\t      die \\\\\\\"timed out after ${wait_s}s waiting for the review lock $dir (holder pid ${holder:-unknown})\\\\\\\"\\\\n   190\\\\t    fi\\\\n   191\\\\t    [ \\\\\\\"$waited\\\\\\\" = 0 ] && log \\\\\\\"another review holds $dir (pid ${holder:-unknown}); queuing\\\\\\\"\\\\n   192\\\\t    sleep 5\\\\n   193\\\\t    waited=$((waited + 5))\\\\n   194\\\\t  done\\\\n   195\\\\t  printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' \\\\\\\"$$\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$dir/pid\\\\\\\"\\\\n   196\\\\t  printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' \\\\\\\"$label\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$dir/label\\\\\\\"\\\\n   197\\\\t  LOCK_HELD=\\\\\\\"$dir\\\\\\\"\\\\n   198\\\\t}\\\\n   199\\\\t\\\\n   200\\\\t# lint_branch_name — the bracket incident (docs/pr_review_management.md:163,\\\\n   201\\\\t# CONTRIBUTING.md:122-124).\\\\n   202\\\\tlint_branch_name() {\\\\n   203\\\\t  case \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" in\\\\n   204\\\\t    \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\") die \\\\\\\"empty branch name in the PR record\\\\\\\" ;;\\\\n   205\\\\t  esac\\\\n   206\\\\t  if printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" | LC_ALL=C grep -q '[]~^:?* \\\\\\\\]'; then\\\\n   207\\\\t    die \\\\\\\"branch name '$1' contains a character that broke the parent automation ( ] ~ ^ : ? * space backslash ); see CONTRIBUTING.md:122-124\\\\\\\"\\\\n   208\\\\t  fi\\\\n   209\\\\t}\\\\n   210\\\\t\\\\n   211\\\\t# fetch_trusted <repo-